<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mozarabs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mozarab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mozarabs were Christians who lived under Muslim rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a> and culture while keeping their faith. Many migrated north to the Christian kingdoms from the 9th century, where they helped resettle frontier lands in León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Portugal. They kept a distinctive liturgy, the Mozarabic rite, and their name also covers the Romance dialects spoken in al-Andalus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mozarabs.jpg" alt="Mozarabs" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fogar Mozárabe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arcos_de_herradura_moz%C3%A1rabe_de_Santiago_de_Pe%C3%B1alba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muslims, many of them descendants of Iberian converts, probably formed the majority by the tenth century; Christians, known as <b>Mozarabs</b>, and Jews lived as protected but subordinate communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the ninth and tenth centuries growing numbers of Christians converted to Islam, becoming muwalladun, while those who remained Christian, later called <b>Mozarabs</b>, became increasingly Arabised.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…and other southern French lords, he took Zaragoza after a siege of about seven months in 1118, defeated an Almoravid army at the Battle of Cutanda in 1120 and in 1125–1126 led a raid deep into Andalusia, returning with thousands of <b>Mozarab Christians</b> to settle his new lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the ninth century the kingdom expanded southwards through organised resettlement, in which peasants, monasteries and nobles took possession of abandoned land by the practice of presura, while Christians from al-Andalus, later called <b>Mozarabs</b>, migrated north and brought churchmen, books and building traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The conquest of Toledo in 1085 brought a large city with Muslim, Jewish and Arabic-speaking Christian (<b>Mozarab</b>) communities under Castilian rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Society comprised nobles, clergy, free peasants and dependent cultivators, with <b>Mozarabs</b>, Arabic-speaking Christians who migrated from al-Andalus bringing their own artistic traditions, and Jewish communities in the towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
